        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the numbers below.</strong> Capacities, roller
          diameters and motor ratings are manufacturers' published figures. Weight capacity in
          particular is a warranty term set by the manufacturer rather than a measured
          engineering limit, and it varies between model years under the same product name.
          Confirm it for the exact unit you are buying, in writing, before ordering. We have not
          tested these machines; what we say about how they behave under load is inferred from
          those published specifications, not from our own measurements.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#capacity-myth">Why the Weight Capacity Figure Misleads</a></li>
            <li><a href="#criteria">The Five Specs That Actually Matter Here</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-we-tested">How We Chose for Heavier Users</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">The Six Best Treadmills for Heavy Users</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty">Warranty Terms Deserve a Careful Read</a></li>
            <li><a href="#maintenance">Maintenance Matters More at Higher Loads</a></li>
            <li><a href="#walking-first">A Note on Starting With Walking</a></li>
            <li><a href="#delivery">Delivery, Floors and Getting It Into the Room</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">Final Recommendations</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
