        <div id="capacity-myth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why the Weight Capacity Figure Misleads</h2>
<p>
            Every treadmill lists a maximum user weight, and almost everyone shopping in this
            category treats it as the primary specification. It is worth understanding what that
            number actually is: a warranty boundary chosen by the manufacturer's legal and
            engineering teams together, above which they will not honour a claim. It is not a
            point at which the machine fails, and it is not a promise that the machine performs
            well anywhere below it.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, a machine rated to 300 lbs is engineered around a user meaningfully
            lighter than that. Run it near its ceiling and several things change at once: the
            deck deflects further on each footfall so the cushioning bottoms out sooner, the
            motor draws more current and runs hotter, the belt experiences more friction against
            the deck, and the rollers take a larger load across a smaller contact area. None of
            those produce an immediate failure. All of them shorten the machine's life and make
            it less pleasant to use from day one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical rule we would give anyone shopping here is to treat the stated capacity
            as though it were 50 to 75 lbs lower than printed. If you weigh 280 lbs, shop
            machines rated 350 lbs and above rather than machines rated 300. That single
            adjustment does more for your experience and the machine's lifespan than any other
            decision on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="criteria" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Five Specs That Actually Matter Here</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Roller diameter.</strong> The most informative and least advertised
            specification in this category. Rollers are the cylinders the belt runs around, and
            their diameter determines how tightly the belt has to flex on every rotation and how
            much load the bearings carry. Budget machines use 1.9-inch rollers; better ones use
            2.5 inches; commercial machines use 3 inches or more. Larger rollers mean less <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt
            wear</a>, less bearing load and less motor strain — and under a heavier user, all three
            matter considerably more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor type and continuous rating.</strong> A <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>DC motor</a> under sustained heavy
            load builds heat, and heat is what kills treadmill motors. AC motors, which appear on
            commercial machines, handle continuous load far better. If you are buying a DC
            machine, 3.5 CHP is a sensible floor rather than a nice extra. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> explains the continuous-versus-peak distinction that matters most here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame mass and deck support.</strong> How many points support the deck, and
            how heavy is the frame? A deck supported at four points flexes between them; a deck
            on a dense support structure does not. Assembled weight is again the best available
            proxy, and in this category we would not look below 250 lbs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck width.</strong> Twenty-two inches rather than twenty. This is a comfort
            and safety specification for heavier users specifically: a wider stance is common,
            and a belt that does not accommodate it means rail strikes and constant small
            corrections.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cushioning recovery, not softness.</strong> The question is not how soft the
            deck feels but whether it returns to shape between footfalls under a heavy load. A
            deck that compresses and stays compressed is one that has run out of travel, and that
            is when you start feeling impact through the frame.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose for Heavier Users</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines, and nothing on this page should be read as if we
            had. Testing this category properly would require testers at the weights involved, and
            we have not done that. Instead, every machine was judged on the published
            specifications that govern how a treadmill copes with load: roller diameter, motor
            type and continuous rating, assembled weight as a proxy for frame mass, deck width
            and stated capacity.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those specifications point in a consistent direction. Larger rollers, an AC motor, a
            heavier frame and a capacity well above the user's weight all mean a machine working
            further inside its limits, and a machine working inside its limits runs cooler, flexes
            less and lasts longer. Where a machine's figures do not tell a consistent story — a
            high capacity on a light frame with small rollers, for instance — we trust the
            supporting figures over the headline.
          </p>
          <p>
            Motor heat deserves particular attention. A DC motor under sustained heavy load, such
            as long daily incline walks at a higher body weight, builds heat, and heat is the
            single most common reason machines in this category fail early. That is a problem a
            showroom visit cannot surface, which is why motor type weighs heavily in the ranking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Six Best Treadmills for Heavy Users</h2>
<p>
            Capacities below are the manufacturers' stated figures. Our recommendations assume
            the 50 to 75 lb margin described above, and each entry says who we would and would
            not put on the machine.
          </p>
        </div>
