        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty Terms Deserve a Careful Read</h2>
<p>
            Warranty terms matter more in this category than in any other, and they are worth
            reading in full rather than skimming the headline number. Two clauses are worth
            finding specifically.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first is whether the warranty is voided by exceeding the stated capacity. Almost
            all of them are, and that is reasonable, but it makes the capacity figure a financial
            matter rather than a comfort one. If two people in a household will use the machine
            and one is above the limit, the warranty position needs to be understood before
            purchase, not after a failure.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second is the distinction between parts, labour and the frame. Frame warranties
            of ten years or lifetime look generous and cover the component least likely to fail.
            What you want to see is a long parts warranty and, ideally, more than one year of
            labour, because the expensive failures in this category are motors and controller
            boards and the labour to replace them is a significant share of the cost. Our <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan and repair guide</a> covers the repair-or-replace arithmetic in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Maintenance Matters More at Higher Loads</h2>
<p>
            Everything in our standard <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>maintenance schedule</a> applies here on a shorter interval.
            Friction between the belt and the deck is the primary cause of motor and controller
            failure, and load increases friction directly — so a heavier user generates the
            failure condition faster.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where our general advice is to lubricate <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>every 40 to 50 hours</a> of use, in this
            category we would treat 30 hours as the interval, and we would check belt tension and
            tracking monthly rather than quarterly. That is perhaps twenty minutes of work a year
            more than a lighter user needs, against a motor replacement that costs several
            hundred dollars.
          </p>
          <p>
            The step-by-step is in our <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>,
            and the full schedule is in the <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance pillar</a>.
            One warning worth repeating: check your manual before applying anything, because some
            decks are pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Note on Starting With Walking</h2>
<p>
            This is an equipment guide rather than a training one, but one point is worth making
            because it changes which machine to buy. Impact force scales with body weight, and
            running generates roughly two and a half to three times body weight per footfall
            against approximately one and a half times for walking. At 300 lbs that is a very
            large difference in load, both on your joints and on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many people shopping in this category will get better results, and a much easier
            first three months, from incline walking than from running — more work done, far less
            impact, and a machine that will last longer for it. If that is the likely use, the
            deck length requirement relaxes and the money is better spent on frame and motor. Our <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill guide</a> and the <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 incline protocol</a> both cover that approach, and the protocol page includes a gentler build-up. Our <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills and weight loss</a> is honest about what training alone does and does not achieve.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Floors and Getting It Into the Room</h2>
<p>
            The machines that suit this category are the heavy ones, and assembled weights of
            250 to 400 lbs create practical problems that are easy to discover too late. Almost
            every treadmill is delivered as kerbside freight by default, which means a pallet on
            your driveway and nothing more. Threshold and room-of-choice delivery are usually
            available as paid upgrades, and for a machine in this weight class they are worth
            buying rather than improvising with friends.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure the route before you order, not just the room. Doorway widths, stairwell
            turns and any landing pivot are where deliveries fail, and a treadmill that arrives
            in one crate cannot be angled through a tight turn the way flat-packed furniture can.
            If the machine is going upstairs, confirm with the retailer whether their delivery
            team will carry it up — many will not above a stated weight, regardless of what you
            have paid for.
          </p>
          <p>
            Floor loading is worth a moment's thought too, though it is rarely a genuine problem.
            A 350 lb machine plus a 300 lb user is 650 lbs spread across four feet, which is
            comparable to a loaded bookcase and well within normal residential floor capacity.
            What does matter is what the machine sits on: a solid, level surface rather than a
            thick carpet, and a proper mat underneath to spread load and stop the frame walking
            under use. Our <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space guide</a> covers the delivery route and clearance requirements in full, and <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">placing a treadmill on carpet</a> explains why carpet is a problem for the motor as well as for stability.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            If the budget allows it, the <strong>Life Fitness T3</strong> is the right answer and
            it is not close. A commercial AC motor, a 400 lb capacity that is genuinely
            engineered rather than nominal, and a commercial frame are exactly what this use case
            needs, and on its specifications it is the machine here we would choose for a 330 lb
            user.
          </p>
          <p>
            At half that money, the <strong>Sole F65</strong> is the value pick: 325 lbs stated,
            heavier construction than its price suggests, and no subscription. For a user up to
            about 260 lbs it is entirely sufficient. The <strong>NordicTrack Commercial 1750</strong> is the choice if you want a large screen and guided content alongside a frame that
            can take the load.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever arrives, halve the lubrication interval and check tension monthly. In this
            category maintenance is not housekeeping — it is the difference between six years and
            two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying for a Heavier User</h2>
          <p>These four account for most of the disappointing purchases in this category, and the first one causes the other three.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Treating the stated capacity as a performance figure</h3>
              <p>It is a warranty boundary, not an engineering limit, and machines operating near it deflect further, run hotter and wear faster from the first session. Shop 50 to 75 lbs above your actual weight. This single adjustment does more for both your experience and the machine's lifespan than anything else on this page.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Never checking roller diameter</h3>
              <p>It is the specification that most affects belt and bearing life under load, and it is almost never advertised. A 1.9-inch roller flexes the belt tightly on every rotation and spins fast to move the belt at all. Find the figure before buying — if a retailer cannot tell you, that is itself informative.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a DC motor for long daily incline sessions</h3>
              <p>DC motors build heat under sustained load and heat is the primary failure mechanism. If you plan 45-minute incline walks most days at a higher body weight, an AC motor is a genuinely different class of component and worth the price step. If sessions will be shorter and flatter, a well-rated DC machine is fine.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Keeping the standard maintenance interval</h3>
              <p>Higher load means more belt-to-deck friction, and friction is what destroys motors and controller boards. Halve the lubrication interval to roughly 30 hours and check tension and tracking monthly. That is about twenty extra minutes a year against a motor replacement costing several hundred dollars.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            Shop 50 to 75 lbs above your weight, check the roller diameter before anything else, and
            treat the maintenance interval as half the standard one. The <strong class="text-white">Life Fitness T3</strong> is the machine we would choose for a
            300 lb user; the <strong class="text-white">Sole F65</strong> is the value answer up to about 260 lbs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Training mostly by walking? The <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">walking treadmill guide</a> covers the machines built for that, and the <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">maintenance schedule</a> is what turns a five-year machine into a ten-year one.
          </p>
        </div>

